  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nThe income tax return filing deadline is not the same as the prescriptive period for assessment. Under Section 203 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), internal revenue taxes must be assessed within three years after the last day prescribed by law for filing the return. If a return is filed beyond the prescribed period, the three-year period is counted from the date the return was actually filed. \n\nThis means that while the filing deadline is a specific date set by the BIR for taxpayers to submit their returns, the prescriptive period for assessment is a separate timeframe that begins after this deadline. Therefore, the two periods are distinct and serve different purposes in tax compliance and enforcement.\n\n### Relevant Facts / Assumptions\nThe factual circumstances have not been fully described. The analysis is based on the question as presented.\n\n### Alternative Interpretations\nNo alternative interpretations identified from the available indexed authorities.\n\n### Position Strength\nPosition strength is preliminary and subject to full fact pattern verification.",
